Nu=za wappui ešun.

Nu apiya ḫapan ōḫḫun.

                                       |  
                                      (@) 

Welcome to Ḫattušumna Laḫḫa! This game is presented entirely in the ancient
Hittite language, which is probably a bit intimidating at first. But don't
panic; just take it slow.

Clicking a word will show you its definition. See if you can use that to figure
out the meaning of the two sentences at the top left. When you think you
understand them, press ↩ to continue.

> 
- - - - -
Nu=za wappui ešun.

Nu apiya ḫapan ōḫḫun.

Verbs: ōḫḫun, pāun
                  auriya                 
                    ✧                    
                                         
                                       |  
                                      (@) 

You probably came up with something like "I was at the riverbank. I saw a river
there." Most Hittite sentences start with a meaningless "connective" word, then
have a series of nouns, and finish off with a verb. The case of those nouns
indicates their purpose: a "dative" noun like wappui indicates a location or
destination, while an "accusative" noun like ḫapan indicates the object of the
verb.

Now it's time to construct a sentence of your own. The compass rose at the top
right indicates nearby locations, and the "verbs" bar suggests some relevant
verbs. Double-click any of these words to insert them into your sentence; see
if you can use them to move to a new location.

> * Starting out
> nu auriya paun
Nu auriya pāun.

- - - - -
Nu=za auriya peran ešun.

Nu apiya ḫattalwantan āškan ōḫḫun.

Verbs: ōḫḫun, pāun, šarraḫḫun
                  āškan                  
              palši ✧ kutti              
                 wappui                  
                                       :  
                                     -(@)-
                                       |  
                                      ( ) 

There we go! You'll encounter many more verbs and nouns as you go along, but
all the relevant ones will be shown at the top of the screen: what you can see
and where you can go. (You can also leave off the sentence connectives if you
want.)

> acki paun
This line is likely a scribal error. The verb pāun "go" is generally used with
locations, not objects; for things like doors and thresholds, the verb 
šarraḫḫun "pass through" is used instead.

> carrahhun acki
The structure of this sentence is unusual. "Šarraḫḫun" is a verb, and the verb
should always be at the end of a Hittite sentence.

> acki carrahhun
This sentence, "āški šarraḫḫun", is likely a scribal error. The verb šarraḫḫun
expects a noun in the accusative, not the dative: āškan rather than āški.

> ackan carrahhun
N=an natta taruḫḫun šarraḫḫun. Nu āškaš ḫattalwanza ēšta.

> palci paun
Nu palši pāun.

- - - - -
Nu=za palši ešun.

Nu apiya ilan ōḫḫun.

