You're getting the hang of this now! Since you'll be moving around a lot in
this game, there's a shorter way to get from place to place: the Babylonians
sometimes numbered their compass directions, 1 south 2 north 3 east 4 west, so
you can type "1 3 2" to go south, then east, then north. These numbers will be
shown on the compass rose in the top right.

There's no evidence that the Hittites did this, and this game addis numbers 5
and 6 for "up" and "down", which is completely non-historical. But the Hittites
imitating Babylonian writing isn't much of a stretch, and typing a full
sentence every time gets tedious very fast! You can also click the map to
navigate to anywhere you've been before.

> nu ackan dahhun
Nu āškan natta taruḫḫun dāḫḫun. N=aš mekki nakkiš ēšta.

> halhaltomari paun
It's unclear what "ḫalḫaltomari" is meant to refer to; this noun is used on the
tablet, but what it describes is not currently near our protagonist.

> kutti paun
Nu kutti pāun.

